# Source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2" (solved), 1 b).
id: square-36
title: A precondition with two solutions
topic: Sequential composition
level: 2
source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 1 b
tags: assignment, sequence, disjunction, consequence

task:
  Show that the triple is valid. Compute the weakest precondition and
  explain why it is weaker than the given precondition.

pre: x = 5
post: x = 36
range: 8

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  y := x + 1;      // i1
  {R1}
  x := y * y       // i2
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The last statement, `x := y * y`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
calc:
  wp(x := y * y, Q)
  = (x = 36)[x <- y * y]
  = y * y = 36
set: R1 = y * y = 36

step: The first statement, `y := x + 1`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(y := x + 1, R1)
  = (x + 1) * (x + 1) = 36
  = x + 1 = 6 || x + 1 = -6       // the square roots of 36 are 6 and -6
  = x = 5 || x = -7
set: R2 = x = 5 || x = -7

step: Compare with the precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x = 5 ==> x = 5 || x = -7
why:
  This is the law $A ==> A || B$: if one side of a disjunction holds, the
  disjunction holds.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The weakest precondition has two solutions, $5$ and $-7$, since
  squaring forgets the sign. The given precondition picks one of them, so
  it is stronger than the weakest precondition, and the consequence rule
  applies.
